          "settings": {
            "number": "1",
            "title": "Why Breastfed Babies Often Struggle With Bottles",
            "body": "<p>A mother’s breast is constantly changing. It <strong>softens, compresses, and adapts</strong> to your baby’s mouth throughout every feeding.</p><p>A standard bottle nipple can’t do that.</p><p>It keeps the same <strong>shape, angle, and flow</strong>, no matter how your baby sucks, pauses, or changes pressure.</p><p>When a nipple is <strong>too long, too firm, or doesn’t fit comfortably</strong>, your baby has to work around the bottle instead of feeding naturally.</p><p>That’s when problems start. <strong>The latch slips. Milk leaks from the corners of the mouth.</strong> A baby who was ready to eat suddenly seems frustrated by the bottle itself.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image-2": {
