        "reason-2": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "2",
            "title": "Why Even the \"Best\" Anti-Colic Bottles Don't Always Work",
            "body": "<p>For years, the bottle industry treated <strong>air bubbles as the main problem</strong>.</p><p>That led to more vents, tubes, valves, and flow-control parts—anything that made a bottle appear more “anti-colic.”</p><p>But most of those designs come from an older idea: improve milk flow, reduce trapped air, and prevent leaks.</p><p>Helpful, but not the full picture.</p><p>For many breastfed babies, the struggle begins <strong>before air ever reaches the stomach</strong>. It starts with <strong>how the nipple fits and sits inside the mouth</strong>.</p><p>That’s why parents can buy a highly rated anti-colic bottle and still feel something is wrong.</p><p>The bottle may be designed to solve <strong>air and bubbles</strong>, while the baby is actually struggling with <strong>latch and nipple shape</strong>.</p>"
          }
        },
